IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `sg-child-support-domain`

§ `Childcare leave — Child Development Co-Savings Act 2001, and what the Rally would do to it`

-- Unlike the money, THIS half of the subject has a statute. Sections 12B,
-- 12C and 12CA of the Child Development Co-Savings Act 2001 are in force
-- and are encoded here in the shape they are written in. The Rally
-- announced that the two leave entitlements would be merged into one of
-- 8, 10 or 12 days by number of children, and that the Government would
-- "cover the cost for all child-related leave ... up to the reimbursement
-- limit". No Bill has been introduced and, on the reporting, "no start
-- date has been announced". So the announced arm below is dated at the
-- Package's commencement as a PLACEHOLDER, and the encoding says so
-- rather than implying the date is known.

§§ `Section 12B(1B) and (1C) — who is an entitled employee at all`

-- The bar is on the natural father of a child conceived while either he or
-- the mother was married to somebody else, and it is lifted if the two of
-- them later marry each other. The Rally said nothing about it, so it is
-- encoded once and carried into both arms: an announcement that does not
-- mention a provision does not repeal it.
@ref CDCSA 2001 s 12B(1B), (1C)
GIVEN parent IS A Parent, `conceived while either was married to another` IS A BOOLEAN, `have since married each other` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is barred from leave for the child` IF
        parent's `is the natural father`
    AND `conceived while either was married to another`
    AND NOT `have since married each other`

@ref CDCSA 2001 s 12B(1)(a), (1A)(a) — three months' service is the threshold
GIVEN parent IS A Parent
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `has served the qualifying period` IF
    parent's `months served this period` AT LEAST 3

§§ `Section 12B(1) — childcare leave, graduated by months served`

-- The graduation is the part the headline drops. The Act does not give
-- six days to everyone who has a young child; it gives two, three, four,
-- five or six according to how much of the relevant period the employee
-- served that employer.
@ref CDCSA 2001 s 12B(1)(i)-(v)
GIVEN months IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the graduated band for` months MEANS
    BRANCH IF months LESS THAN 3  THEN 0
           IF months LESS THAN 5  THEN 2
           IF months LESS THAN 7  THEN 3
           IF months LESS THAN 9  THEN 4
           IF months LESS THAN 11 THEN 5
           OTHERWISE 6

§§ `Counting the children each entitlement turns on`

GIVEN xs IS A LIST OF Child
GIVETH A NUMBER
`how many` xs MEANS foldr (GIVEN x, acc YIELD acc PLUS 1) 0 xs

@ref CDCSA 2001 s 12B(1)(b) — a qualifying child below 7 years of age
GIVEN parent IS A Parent, day IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`children below seven` parent day MEANS
    `how many` (filter (GIVEN c YIELD     c's `is a Singapore citizen`
                                      AND `the age` c `has on` day LESS THAN 7)
                       (parent's `children`))

@ref CDCSA 2001 s 12B(1A)(b) — of or above 7 but below 13 years of age
GIVEN parent IS A Parent, day IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`children from seven to twelve` parent day MEANS
    `how many` (filter (GIVEN c YIELD     c's `is a Singapore citizen`
                                      AND `the age` c `has on` day AT LEAST 7
                                      AND `the age` c `has on` day LESS THAN 13)
                       (parent's `children`))

-- The announced scheme bands on one number: Singapore Citizen children
-- "aged 12 and below", which is the union of the Act's two age bands.
@ref NDR 2026 — children aged 12 and below
GIVEN parent IS A Parent, day IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`children twelve and below` parent day MEANS
         `children below seven` parent day
    PLUS `children from seven to twelve` parent day

§§ `The entitlement under the Act as it stands`

-- s 12B(2)(a)(iii): a COMBINED total of six days in any relevant period,
-- however many children the employee has and whatever their ages. The
-- combined cap is what makes the Act's answer independent of family size
-- — which is precisely the feature the Rally set out to change.
@ref CDCSA 2001 s 12B(1), (1A), (2)(a)(iii)
GIVEN parent IS A Parent, day IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`days of leave under the Act` parent day MEANS
    BRANCH IF NOT `has served the qualifying period` parent THEN 0
           IF `the combined entitlement` AT MOST 6 THEN `the combined entitlement`
           OTHERWISE 6
    WHERE
        `the childcare leave limb` MEANS
            IF   `children below seven` parent day AT LEAST 1
            THEN `the graduated band for` (parent's `months served this period`)
            ELSE 0
        `the extended limb` MEANS
            IF   `children from seven to twelve` parent day AT LEAST 1
            THEN 2
            ELSE 0
        `the combined entitlement` MEANS `the childcare leave limb` PLUS `the extended limb`

§§ `The entitlement under the announced scheme`

-- "Every working parent will get: 8 days with one child, aged 12 and
-- below, 10 days with two children, aged 12 and below, and 12 days with
-- three or more children."
@ref NDR 2026 (23 Aug 2026) — the merged childcare leave scheme
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the announced entitlement for this many children` n MEANS
    BRANCH IF n AT MOST 0 THEN 0
           IF n EQUALS 1  THEN 8
           IF n EQUALS 2  THEN 10
           OTHERWISE 12

-- FORK F2. The announcement states one number per family size and says
-- nothing about an employee who has served only part of the relevant
-- period. Two readings survive:
--
--   live      — the s 12B(1) graduation survives the merger, because an
--               announcement that does not mention a mechanism does not
--               repeal it, and the days scale in the proportion the Act
--               already uses. THE ROUNDING IS THE ENCODING'S OWN: the
--               source gives no rule for a fractional day, so this arm
--               floors. Any implementation would have to choose, and the
--               choice is visible here rather than buried.
--   rival     — the merged entitlement is flat for everyone past the
--               three-month threshold, which is what the words say.
--
-- Both execute; the case suite shows what turns on the difference.
GIVEN parent IS A Parent, day IS A DATE, reading IS AN Interpretation
GIVETH A NUMBER
`days of leave as announced` parent day reading MEANS
    BRANCH IF NOT `has served the qualifying period` parent THEN 0
           IF reading's `the service-duration graduation survives the merger`
              THEN FLOOR ((full TIMES `the graduated band for` (parent's `months served this period`)) DIVIDED BY 6)
           OTHERWISE full
    WHERE
        full MEANS `the announced entitlement for this many children` (`children twelve and below` parent day)

§§ `The entitlement, on whichever arm the rule date selects`

GIVEN parent IS A Parent, day IS A DATE, reading IS AN Interpretation
GIVETH A NUMBER
`days of childcare leave` parent day reading MEANS
    IF   `the rules in force include` `the announced leave scheme commences`
    THEN `days of leave as announced` parent day reading
    ELSE `days of leave under the Act` parent day

-- PLACEHOLDER, AND LABELLED ONE. The merged scheme needs an amendment to
-- the Act and no commencement date has been announced. This encoding dates
-- the announced arm at the Package's own commencement so that the arm can
-- be reached and tested at all; it is NOT a claim that the leave scheme
-- starts on that day. Fork register F3 records the assumption.
@ref NDR 2026 — merged leave scheme; no commencement date announced (placeholder)
GIVETH A DATE
`the announced leave scheme commences` MEANS YMD 2027 4 1

§§ `Who actually pays — sections 12B(9)-(10A), 12C and 12CA`

-- The Act splits the cost three ways, and the split is the part the
-- headline "the Government will cover the cost" has to be read against.
--
--   s 12B(9)   the employer pays the employee's GROSS RATE OF PAY for
--              every day of leave taken;
--   s 12B(10)  after three days of childcare leave, that payment "must not
--              exceed $500 for each subsequent day";
--   s 12B(10A) every day of EXTENDED childcare leave is capped at $500;
--   s 12C      the employer may reclaim days four to six of childcare
--              leave, at not more than $500 a day and $1,500 a year;
--   s 12CA     and up to two days of extended childcare leave, at not more
--              than $500 a day and $1,000 a year.
--
-- So today the employer bears the first three days in full and the
-- Government bears the rest — capped. The Rally said the Government would
-- "cover the cost for all child-related leave ... UP TO THE REIMBURSEMENT
-- LIMIT", and the reported limit is unchanged at $500 a day. The four
-- words after "all" are the whole finding: for any parent whose gross
-- daily rate exceeds $500, the employer keeps paying the excess, on every
-- day, and the day count doubles.
@ref CDCSA 2001 s 12C(2)(a), s 12CA(2)(a); NDR 2026 reporting — $500 a day
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the daily reimbursement limit` MEANS 500

GIVEN a IS A NUMBER, b IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the lesser of` a `and` b MEANS IF a AT MOST b THEN a ELSE b

GIVEN a IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`no less than nothing` a MEANS IF a AT MOST 0 THEN 0 ELSE a

@ref CDCSA 2001 s 12B(9)-(10A) read with s 12C(1) — the employer bears the first three days
GIVEN parent IS A Parent, day IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the employer's cost under the Act` parent day MEANS
    `the lesser of` (`days of leave under the Act` parent day) `and` 3
    TIMES parent's `gross daily rate`

@ref NDR 2026 — the Government covers all child-related leave up to the reimbursement limit
GIVEN parent IS A Parent, day IS A DATE, reading IS AN Interpretation
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the employer's cost if announced` parent day reading MEANS
         `days of leave as announced` parent day reading
    TIMES `no less than nothing` (parent's `gross daily rate` MINUS `the daily reimbursement limit`)

-- The number an employer actually wants: does the reform cost them more or
-- less? Negative is a saving.
GIVEN parent IS A Parent, day IS A DATE, reading IS AN Interpretation
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the change to the employer's cost` parent day reading MEANS
         `the employer's cost if announced` parent day reading
    MINUS `the employer's cost under the Act` parent day

-- The gross daily rate at which the reform stops saving an employer money.
-- Below it the employer pays less than today; above it, more. It is a
-- consequence of the encoding, not a figure anybody announced.
GIVEN parent IS A Parent, day IS A DATE, reading IS AN Interpretation
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the reform costs this employer more money` parent day reading IF
    `the change to the employer's cost` parent day reading GREATER THAN 0

§§ `Section 12B(8) — the obligation, and how the entitlement lapses`

DECLARE LeaveActor IS ONE OF
    `the employer`
    `the employee`

DECLARE LeaveAction IS ONE OF
    `grant the leave` HAS days IS A NUMBER
    `take the leave`  HAS days IS A NUMBER

-- "an employer must grant, and an employee who is entitled to childcare
-- leave or extended childcare leave must take, the entitlement ... not
-- later than the last day of that relevant period, and any employee who
-- fails to take that leave by that day (a) thereupon ceases to be entitled
-- to that leave; and (b) is not entitled to any payment in lieu of that
-- leave."
--
-- The lapse is the reason this is a regulative rule and not a number: the
-- entitlement is use-it-or-lose-it, so an encoding that reported only the
-- day count would tell a parent in December something materially
-- misleading.
@ref CDCSA 2001 s 12B(8)
GIVEN days IS A NUMBER, `days left in the relevant period` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC LeaveActor LeaveAction
`the leave must be granted and taken` days `with` `days left in the relevant period` MEANS
    PARTY  `the employer`
    MUST   (`grant the leave` days)
    WITHIN `days left in the relevant period`
    HENCE  (PARTY  `the employee`
            MUST   (`take the leave` days)
            WITHIN `days left in the relevant period`
            HENCE  FULFILLED
            LEST   BREACH BY `the employee`
                   BECAUSE "CDCSA s 12B(8): leave not taken by the last day of the relevant period ceases to be an entitlement, and no payment in lieu is due")
    LEST   BREACH BY `the employer`
           BECAUSE "CDCSA s 12B(8): an employer must grant the entitlement not later than the last day of the relevant period"
